<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Lusignan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Lusignan was a French noble family from Poitou, lords of Lusignan and counts of La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a>. Through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a> it gained the crowns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> at the end of the 12th century, and it ruled Cyprus until the late 15th century. A branch inherited the throne of the Armenian Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> in 1342, and the family kept the title King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> after its fall in 1375.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-lusignan.jpg" alt="House of Lusignan" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James2813 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_the_House_of_Lusignan_(Kings_of_Armenia,_Cyprus_and_Jerusalem).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
